<nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> › Labs</nav>
<h1>Well water testing labs by state</h1>
<p>Pick your state. Each page says what the source list is, how old it is, and what we could not confirm.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Labs listed</th><th>Source list</th><th>Checked</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
{% for key, d in labs %}
<tr><td><a href="/labs/{{ key }}/">{{ states[key].name }}</a></td>
<td>{{ (d.labs | groupFilter('main')).length }} main{% if (d.labs | groupFilter('public')).length %} + {{ (d.labs | groupFilter('public')).length }} utility/agency{% endif %}{% if (d.labs | groupFilter('limited')).length %} + {{ (d.labs | groupFilter('limited')).length }} limited{% endif %}</td>
<td><a href="{{ d.list_source.page_url }}" rel="noopener">{{ d.list_source.name }}</a><br><span class="small">{{ d.list_source.report_date }}</span></td>
<td>{{ d.last_verified }}</td></tr>
{% endfor %}
</tbody></table>
<p class="small">Counts are split into main (commercial and independent labs), utility/agency labs that mostly serve their own customers, and limited-scope labs (for example, a single test, or out-of-state labs). The groups follow each state's own list; where we sorted labs by name, the state page says so. New York is limited to the labs registered with Westchester County; use the state's ELAP search for the rest of the state. See the <a href="/labs/new-york/">New York page</a>.</p>
<p class="small">Not listed yet: states whose official lab list is only an interactive search (for example New Jersey and Massachusetts) or is not published as a list we could verify. We would rather leave a state out than guess.</p>
